From 1b8b7c28006bf3664d32f8534d4faedc21a08c48 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: perf <perf@local>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 12:01:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH giallo] perf: give each thread its own RegSet instead of
 locking one

`PatternSet` held `Option<Mutex<RegSet>>` because `onig_regset_search` writes
to the regset's internal region storage, so one instance cannot be searched
concurrently. Pattern sets are shared through `Arc` from the registry's cache,
so every thread highlighting the same language queues behind that one lock.

Measured in Zola (3776-page and synthetic sites, 12-core M4 Pro): highlighting
did not parallelise at all - the markdown phase took 1.58 s on one thread and
1.69 s on twelve.

Keep the pattern strings and compile a `RegSet` per thread on first use,
stored in a thread-local map keyed by a per-pattern-set id. Only a thread that
actually searches a given set pays for a copy.

After, same machine, markdown phase:

  threads   1      2       4       12
  before    1.58s  ~1.6s   ~1.6s   1.69s
  after     1.58s  0.94s   0.56s   0.27s   (5.9x, efficiency 0.49)

Whole-build wall time in Zola: markdown-heavy 2000 pages 2.25 s -> 0.87 s,
4000 pages 4.24 s -> 1.69 s. Peak RSS grows by 5-8 MB.

Highlighted output is byte-identical: Zola's output-equivalence check reports
IDENTICAL on markdown-heavy-1000/4000, template-heavy-1000 and
mixed-realistic-1000, and two runs of the patched binary agree with each other.

Note on lifetime: entries are keyed by id and live until the thread exits, so a
process that keeps creating registries (`zola serve` reloading a config) grows
this map. Storing a `thread_local::ThreadLocal<RegSet>` inside `PatternSet`
would tie the storage to the object instead, at the cost of one dependency.
